select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 7, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.0", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.0 Overview", "body": "Burghardt, G., and Belton, T. (2005). The Treasury Bond Basis (3rd ed.). McGraw-Hill.\nCME Group. Three-Month SOFR Futures: Contract Specifications and U.S. Treasury Bond Futures: Contract Specifications. Chicago: CME Group.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 6.\nTuckman, B., and Serrat, A. (2022). Fixed Income Securities: Tools for Today's Markets (4th ed.). Wiley."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.0", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nIn which market should each company borrow?\nTwo companies each need $100,000,000 for five years. Alder Corporation, which has the higher credit rating, wants to pay a floating rate. Birch Corporation wants to pay a fixed rate. Lenders offer the terms below. Select the arrangement that gives the lower total cost.\nBorrowing rates offered to the two companies\nCompanyFixed rateFloating rateWants to pay\nAlder Corporation4.00%SOFR + 0.20%Floating\nBirch Corporation5.40%SOFR + 0.90%Fixed"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.1", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.1", "heading": "The mechanics of an interest rate swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.1 The mechanics of an interest rate swap", "body": "Chapter 1 defined a swap as an agreement between two parties to exchange cash flows on a series of future dates according to a specified rule. In an interest rate swap, one party pays interest at a fixed rate on a notional principal, and the other pays interest at a floating rate on the same principal, in the same currency, for a specified number of years. The principal is called notional because it is not exchanged; it serves only to determine the size of the interest payments. The party that pays the fixed rate is the fixed-rate payer; the other is the fixed-rate receiver, or floating-rate payer.\nThe floating rate in most swaps is now an overnight reference rate — SOFR, SONIA, or €STR — compounded daily over each payment period. A swap of a fixed rate for a compounded overnight rate is an overnight indexed swap (OIS). Because the compounded rate for a period is known only at the end of that period, the floating payment is determined, and paid, at the end of each period. Only the difference between the two interest amounts is paid, by whichever party owes the larger."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.1", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.1", "heading": "The mechanics of an interest rate swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.1 The mechanics of an interest rate swap", "body": "The fixed rate that makes a swap of a given maturity have zero value at inception is the swap rate for that maturity. Dealers quote a bid rate, the fixed rate they will pay in exchange for receiving floating, and a slightly higher offer rate, the fixed rate they require in exchange for paying floating. The swap rate is the average of the two, and the difference between them is the dealer's compensation."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.1", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.1", "heading": "An example: Northwind's swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.1 An example: Northwind's swap", "body": "Northwind Components has a three-year bank loan of $50,000,000 on which it pays interest every six months at compounded SOFR plus 1.20 percent. It wishes to fix its interest cost. It enters a three-year swap with a notional principal of $50,000,000 under which it pays a fixed rate of 4.20 percent per year and receives compounded SOFR, with payments every six months. Northwind then has three sets of cash flows:\nit pays SOFR + 1.20 percent to the bank under the loan;\nit receives SOFR under the swap;\nit pays 4.20 percent under the swap.\nThe two SOFR amounts cancel, and the net cost is \\(4.20+1.20=5.40\\) percent per year, fixed, whatever SOFR proves to be. The swap has transformed a floating-rate liability into a fixed-rate liability. Interactive figure 7.1 sets out the cash flows period by period; for simplicity each period is treated as exactly half a year."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.1#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.1", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.1", "heading": "An example: Northwind's swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.1 An example: Northwind's swap", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 7.1 — Cash flows of a swap and a loan\nA floating-rate loan and a pay-fixed swap produce a fixed cost\nThe table shows six semiannual periods. Generate a new path for SOFR and confirm, in each row, that the net receipt under the swap offsets the change in the interest on the loan, leaving a total of $1,350,000, which is 5.40 percent per year on $50,000,000."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.1#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.1", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.1", "heading": "An example: Northwind's swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.1 An example: Northwind's swap", "body": "A swap can equally transform an asset. An investor who holds a bond paying a fixed rate and who expects rates to rise can enter a swap to pay fixed and receive floating, converting the fixed income into a floating income. An investor holding a floating-rate asset can receive fixed and pay floating to obtain a fixed income.\nSwaps are documented under the Master Agreement of the International Swaps and Derivatives Association (ISDA), which sets out the terms that apply to all transactions between two parties, including what happens if one of them defaults. The particular terms of each trade are recorded in a short confirmation.\nConcept check 7.1\nA company has issued a five-year bond paying a fixed rate of 5.0 percent. It now believes that rates will fall and wishes to pay a floating rate. Five-year swaps are quoted at 4.3 percent against SOFR. What should it do, and what will it pay?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.2", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.2", "heading": "The comparative-advantage argument", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.2 The comparative-advantage argument", "body": "A long-standing explanation of the popularity of swaps is that of comparative advantage. A company is said to have a comparative advantage in the fixed-rate market if the premium it pays over a more creditworthy borrower is smaller in the floating-rate market than in the fixed-rate market, and conversely. In the opening exercise, Birch pays 1.40 percentage points more than Alder for fixed-rate funds and only 0.70 points more for floating-rate funds. Birch pays more in both markets, so Alder has an absolute advantage in both; but Birch's disadvantage is smaller in the floating-rate market, where it therefore has a comparative advantage, and Alder has a comparative advantage in the fixed-rate market.\nEach company wants to borrow in the market in which it does not have a comparative advantage. The argument holds that each should borrow where its comparative advantage lies and then use a swap to obtain the kind of rate it wants. The total gain available is the difference between the two premiums:\n\\[ 1.40\\%-0.70\\%=0.70\\%\\ \\text{per year.} \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.2", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.2", "heading": "The comparative-advantage argument", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.2 The comparative-advantage argument", "body": "Worked example 7.1 — Designing the swap\nA swap dealer stands between Alder and Birch and retains 0.10 percent per year. The remaining gain is divided equally. Design the swap.\n1\nDivision of the gain. \\(0.70-0.10=0.60\\) percent, or 0.30 percent to each company. Alder's target cost is \\((\\text{SOFR}+0.20)-0.30=\\text{SOFR}-0.10\\) percent. Birch's target cost is \\(5.40-0.30=5.10\\) percent.\n2\nBorrowings. Alder borrows at the fixed rate of 4.00 percent. Birch borrows at SOFR + 0.90 percent.\n3\nAlder's swap. Alder pays SOFR to the dealer and receives a fixed rate \\(X\\). Its net cost is \\(4.00+\\text{SOFR}-X\\), which equals \\(\\text{SOFR}-0.10\\) when \\(X=4.10\\) percent.\n4\nBirch's swap. Birch receives SOFR from the dealer and pays a fixed rate \\(Y\\). Its net cost is \\((\\text{SOFR}+0.90)-\\text{SOFR}+Y\\), which equals 5.10 when \\(Y=4.20\\) percent.\n5\nThe dealer. The dealer receives 4.20 percent from Birch and pays 4.10 percent to Alder, and passes SOFR from Alder to Birch: a net 0.10 percent per year, or $100,000 on $100,000,000. The dealer's bid is 4.10 and its offer 4.20."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.2", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.2", "heading": "Criticism of the argument", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.2 Criticism of the argument", "body": "If such gains were freely available, the trading that exploited them would be expected to eliminate the difference between the two premiums. That the difference persists calls for an explanation, and the explanation shows that the gain is partly apparent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.2", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.2", "heading": "Criticism of the argument", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.2 Criticism of the argument", "body": "The two premiums are not comparable, because the contracts are different. The fixed rates of 4.00 and 5.40 percent are fixed for five years. The floating rates are quoted as a spread over SOFR, and that spread is typically guaranteed only for a short period; the lender may review it, or decline to renew the loan, if the borrower's credit standing declines. A company with a lower rating is more likely to deteriorate over five years than over six months, which is why its premium for a five-year fixed commitment exceeds its premium for a short-term commitment. In the example, Birch obtains what appears to be a five-year fixed rate of 5.10 percent, but only so long as it can continue to borrow at SOFR + 0.90 percent. If its spread were to rise to SOFR + 2.00 percent, its cost would rise to 6.20 percent. It has fixed the level of interest rates and has not fixed its own credit spread. A direct fixed-rate loan at 5.40 percent would have fixed both."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.3", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.3", "heading": "Determining the swap rate", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.3 Determining the swap rate", "body": "The swap rate can be computed from discount factors by the method used for the par yield in Section 3.3. Consider a swap with notional principal \\(L\\) and payment dates \\(t_1,\\dots,t_n\\), spaced \\(\\tau\\) years apart. Let \\(d_i\\) be the risk-free discount factor for date \\(t_i\\) and \\(s\\) the fixed rate.\nThe fixed leg. The fixed payments are \\(Ls\\tau\\) on each date, and their present value is \\(Ls\\tau\\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_i\\).\nThe floating leg. An amount \\(L\\) invested at the compounded overnight rate, with the interest withdrawn at the end of each period, produces exactly the floating payments of the swap and returns \\(L\\) at \\(t_n\\). The investment costs \\(L\\) today. The floating payments alone are therefore worth \\(L\\) less the present value of the principal returned: \\(L-Ld_n=L(1-d_n)\\).\nResult 7.1 — The swap rate"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.3", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.3", "heading": "Determining the swap rate", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.3 Determining the swap rate", "body": "Equating the values of the two legs, \\[ s=\\frac{1-d_n}{\\tau\\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_i}. \\] At inception a swap at this rate has zero value. At a later date, when \\(n\\) payments remain and the next floating period is about to begin, the value of the swap to the fixed-rate payer is \\[ V=L\\,(1-d_n)-L\\,K\\,\\tau\\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_i, \\] where \\(K\\) is the fixed rate of the swap and the discount factors are those then prevailing. The value to the fixed-rate receiver is \\(-V\\).\nFor the discount factors of Table 3.1 — 0.980199, 0.956954, 0.931928, and 0.906649 at six-month intervals — the two-year swap rate with semiannual payments is \\((1-0.906649)/(0.5\\times3.775729)=4.945\\) percent, the same figure as the two-year par yield found in Section 3.3. The agreement is general: the swap rate is the par yield of the risk-free curve, because a fixed-rate receiver in a swap is in the position of an investor who owns a par bond and has financed it at the floating rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.3", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.3", "heading": "Determining the swap rate", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.3 Determining the swap rate", "body": "A swap can also be regarded as a portfolio of forward rate agreements, one for each period. Valuing each as in Result 6.1, by assuming that forward rates are realized, and summing gives the same value as Result 7.1. When the swap is entered, the sum is zero, although the individual FRAs are not: if the zero curve slopes upward, the fixed-rate payer expects to pay in the early periods and to receive in the later ones."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.4#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.4", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.4", "heading": "Valuing an interest rate swap", "kind": "example", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.4 Valuing an interest rate swap", "body": "Worked example 7.2 — The value of Northwind's swap after eighteen months\nEighteen months after Northwind entered its swap, interest rates have risen. The swap has three semiannual payments remaining. Discount factors for six, twelve, and eighteen months are 0.9760, 0.9510, and 0.9255. Find the value of the swap to Northwind, which pays 4.20 percent on $50,000,000.\n1\nFloating leg. \\(L(1-d_n)=50{,}000{,}000\\times(1-0.9255)=\\$3{,}725{,}000\\).\n2\nFixed leg. \\(\\sum d_i=0.9760+0.9510+0.9255=2.8525\\). \\(LK\\tau\\sum d_i=50{,}000{,}000\\times0.042\\times0.5\\times2.8525=\\$2{,}995{,}125\\).\n3\nValue. \\(V=3{,}725{,}000-2{,}995{,}125=+\\$729{,}875\\) to Northwind.\n4\nInterpretation. The current swap rate for eighteen months is \\((1-0.9255)/(0.5\\times2.8525)=5.2235\\) percent. Northwind pays only 4.20 percent. The saving of 1.0235 percent per year on $50,000,000 for three half-year periods, discounted, is \\(50{,}000{,}000\\times0.010235\\times0.5\\times2.8525=\\$729{,}883\\), which agrees with the value found apart from rounding."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.4", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.4", "heading": "Valuing an interest rate swap", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.4 Valuing an interest rate swap", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 7.2 — The value of a swap as rates change\nA pay-fixed swap gains when rates rise\nThe figure values Northwind's swap, with eighteen months remaining, as all zero rates shift up or down from the levels of Worked example 7.2. Find the shift at which the swap has zero value, and compare the swap rate at that point with 4.20 percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.4", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.4", "heading": "Valuing an interest rate swap", "kind": "warning", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.4 Valuing an interest rate swap", "body": "Common error\nThe value of a swap is not its notional principal, nor any simple fraction of it. Northwind's swap has a notional principal of $50,000,000 and, after a substantial rise in rates, a value of about $730,000, or 1.5 percent of notional. At inception its value was zero. Statements about the size of the swap market that cite notional amounts should be read with this in mind, as Section 1.2 observed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.5#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.5", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.5", "heading": "Currency swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.5 Currency swaps", "body": "A fixed-for-fixed currency swap is an agreement to exchange interest payments and principal in one currency for interest payments and principal in another. Unlike an interest rate swap, a currency swap involves the exchange of principal: at the start, the two parties exchange principal amounts that are equal in value at the spot exchange rate; during the life of the swap, each pays interest at a fixed rate on the principal it received; and at the end the principal amounts are exchanged back, at the original amounts.\nSuppose that Northwind has borrowed $22,000,000 for three years in the United States at 4.5 percent but needs euros to build a plant in Germany, whose revenue will be in euros. The spot exchange rate is $1.10 per euro. Northwind enters a three-year currency swap with a bank under which it pays 3.0 percent per year on €20,000,000 and receives 4.5 percent per year on $22,000,000, with annual payments.\nTable 7.1 Cash flows to Northwind under the currency swap (millions)\nDateDollarsEurosNature\nStart−22.00+20.00Exchange of principal\nYear 1+0.99−0.60Interest\nYear 2+0.99−0.60Interest\nYear 3+0.99−0.60Interest\nYear 3+22.00−20.00Re-exchange of principal"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.5#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.5", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.5", "heading": "Currency swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.5 Currency swaps", "body": "The dollars received under the swap service the dollar borrowing exactly. What remains is a liability to pay 3.0 percent on €20,000,000 and to repay €20,000,000 in three years, which the euro revenue of the plant will meet. The swap has transformed a dollar liability into a euro liability."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.5", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.5", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.5 Valuation", "body": "To the party that receives dollars and pays the foreign currency, the swap is equivalent to owning a dollar bond and having issued a foreign-currency bond. If \\(B_D\\) is the value in dollars of the dollar cash flows remaining, \\(B_F\\) the value in the foreign currency of the foreign cash flows remaining, and \\(S_0\\) the spot price of the foreign currency in dollars, then\n\\[ V=B_D-S_0\\,B_F . \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.5", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.5", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "example", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.5 Valuation", "body": "Worked example 7.3 — Valuing the currency swap after one year\nOne year after the swap of Table 7.1 began, the exchange rate is $1.15 per euro. Risk-free rates are 4.0 percent in dollars and 2.5 percent in euros, with continuous compounding, for all maturities. Two annual payments remain. Find the value of the swap to Northwind.\n1\nDollar leg. \\(B_D=0.99e^{-0.04}+22.99e^{-0.08}=0.9512+21.2224=\\$22.1736\\) million.\n2\nEuro leg. \\(B_F=0.60e^{-0.025}+20.60e^{-0.05}=0.5852+19.5953=\\)€20.1805 million.\n3\nValue. \\(V=22.1736-1.15\\times20.1805=22.1736-23.2076=-\\$1.0340\\) million.\n4\nInterpretation. The euro has appreciated from $1.10 to $1.15, which raises the dollar value of the euros Northwind must pay. The loss on the swap is matched by the higher dollar value of the plant's euro revenue, which was the purpose of the arrangement."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.5", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.5", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.5 Valuation", "body": "The same value is obtained by treating the swap as a set of forward foreign exchange contracts, one for each remaining exchange, and valuing each at the forward rate \\(S_0e^{(r-r_f)T}\\) of Result 4.5. For the first exchange, the forward rate is \\(1.15e^{0.015}=1.1674\\); for the second, \\(1.15e^{0.030}=1.1850\\). Then \\((0.99-0.60\\times1.1674)e^{-0.04}+(22.99-20.60\\times1.1850)e^{-0.08}=-\\$1.0340\\) million.\nA currency swap has zero value at inception but is far more likely than an interest rate swap to acquire a large value, because the final exchange of principal is exposed to the whole of the movement in the exchange rate. Other forms exist: in a fixed-for-floating currency swap one of the two rates floats, and in a floating-for-floating currency swap, also called a cross-currency basis swap, both do.\nConcept check 7.2\nUnder a currency swap a company receives 6 percent on 10 million pounds and pays 4 percent on 13 million dollars, with principal to be re-exchanged at maturity. If the pound depreciates sharply against the dollar during the life of the swap, the value of the swap to the company:"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.6#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.6", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.6", "heading": "Credit risk and other swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.6 Credit risk and other swaps", "body": "A party to a swap is exposed to the default of its counterparty only when the swap has positive value to itself. If the counterparty defaults when the swap has negative value to the surviving party, that party still owes the value to the defaulter's estate and loses nothing. The potential loss is therefore the positive value of the swap, a small fraction of the notional principal for an interest rate swap, in which no principal is exchanged. The exposure of a currency swap is larger, because of the final exchange of principal.\nSince 2009 this exposure has been reduced further. Standardized interest rate swaps between financial institutions are cleared through central counterparties, with initial margin and daily variation margin as described in Section 2.4. Swaps that are not cleared are generally subject to collateral agreements under which the party for which the swap has negative value posts collateral equal to that value."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.6#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.6", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.6", "heading": "Credit risk and other swaps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.6 Credit risk and other swaps", "body": "The principle of a swap extends to any pair of cash-flow streams. An amortizing swap has a notional principal that declines on a schedule, to match a loan that is repaid in instalments. A basis swap exchanges one floating rate for another. In an equity swap, the total return on a share or an index, dividends and capital gains together, is exchanged for a fixed or floating rate of interest; the receiver of the equity return obtains the exposure of an investment in the shares without owning them. In a commodity swap, a fixed price for a quantity of a commodity is exchanged each period for the market price, which gives a producer or user the equivalent of a series of forward contracts at a single price. The credit default swap, in which periodic payments are exchanged for a payment contingent on a default, is the subject of Chapter 17. An option to enter a swap is a swaption.\nConcept check 7.3\nA bank is the fixed-rate receiver in an uncollateralized interest rate swap with a corporation. Interest rates have risen substantially since the swap began. If the corporation now defaults, the bank's loss on the swap is:"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:7.6#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:7.6", "chapter": 7, "section": "7.6", "heading": "Credit risk and other swaps", "kind": "notes", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — 7.6 Credit risk and other swaps", "body": "Historical note\nThe transaction usually identified as the first modern swap was a currency swap arranged by Salomon Brothers in 1981 between the World Bank and IBM. IBM had outstanding debt in Swiss francs and German marks and wished to convert it into dollars after those currencies had fallen against the dollar; the World Bank wished to borrow in those currencies, in which interest rates were low, but had reached the limit of what it could raise directly in those markets. The World Bank borrowed dollars and exchanged the obligations with IBM.\nInterest rate swaps followed within a year. The market grew from a few billion dollars of notional principal in the early 1980s to become the largest of all derivatives markets: at the end of June 2025 the Bank for International Settlements reported that interest rate contracts made up the greater part of the $846 trillion of over-the-counter derivatives outstanding."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Summary#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Summary", "chapter": 7, "section": "Summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — Summary Summary", "body": "In an interest rate swap, a fixed rate is exchanged for a floating rate on a notional principal that is not itself exchanged. A pay-fixed swap converts a floating-rate liability into a fixed-rate liability; a receive-fixed swap does the reverse.\nThe comparative-advantage argument divides the difference between two borrowers' fixed-rate and floating-rate premiums. The gain is partly apparent, because a floating-rate spread is not guaranteed for the life of the swap.\nThe swap rate is \\(s=(1-d_n)/(\\tau\\sum d_i)\\), the par yield of the risk-free curve. The value of a swap to the fixed-rate payer is \\(L(1-d_n)-LK\\tau\\sum d_i\\).\nA currency swap exchanges principal and interest in two currencies and is valued as the difference between two bonds, \\(B_D-S_0B_F\\), or as a set of forward exchange contracts.\nThe credit exposure of a swap is its positive value and not its notional principal; central clearing and collateral reduce it further."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 7, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — Exercises Exercises", "body": "7.1 A company pays a fixed rate of 3.80 percent and receives compounded SOFR on a notional principal of $80,000,000, with semiannual payments. In a period in which compounded SOFR is 4.35 percent, what payment is made, and by whom?\n\\(80{,}000{,}000\\times(0.0435-0.0380)\\times0.5=\\$220{,}000\\), received by the company, since the floating rate exceeds the fixed rate it pays.\n7.2 An investor holds $30,000,000 of a floating-rate note paying SOFR + 0.60 percent and wishes to earn a fixed rate for four years. Four-year swaps are quoted at 4.05 percent bid and 4.09 percent offer. Describe the swap and find the fixed rate earned.\nThe investor pays SOFR and receives fixed. A customer receiving fixed deals at the dealer's bid, 4.05 percent. Net income: \\((\\text{SOFR}+0.60)-\\text{SOFR}+4.05=4.65\\) percent.\n7.3 Company X can borrow at a fixed rate of 5.0 percent or at SOFR + 0.4 percent. Company Y can borrow at a fixed rate of 6.6 percent or at SOFR + 1.2 percent. X wants a floating rate and Y a fixed rate. A dealer requires 0.2 percent. Design a swap that is equally attractive to both companies."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 7, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — Exercises Exercises", "body": "Fixed premium 1.6; floating premium 0.8; total gain 0.8; after the dealer's 0.2, each company gains 0.3. X borrows fixed at 5.0 and aims at SOFR + 0.1; Y borrows at SOFR + 1.2 and aims at 6.3. X pays SOFR and receives fixed \\(X\\): \\(5.0+\\text{SOFR}-X=\\text{SOFR}+0.1\\) gives \\(X=4.9\\). Y receives SOFR and pays fixed \\(Y\\): \\(1.2+Y=6.3\\) gives \\(Y=5.1\\). The dealer earns \\(5.1-4.9=0.2\\).\n7.4 Discount factors for one, two, and three years are 0.9615, 0.9200, and 0.8763. Find the three-year swap rate with annual payments.\n\\(s=(1-0.8763)/(1\\times(0.9615+0.9200+0.8763))=0.1237/2.7578=4.485\\) percent.\n7.5 A bank receives a fixed rate of 5.00 percent and pays compounded SOFR on $100,000,000, with annual payments and exactly two years remaining. Discount factors for one and two years are 0.9620 and 0.9230. Find the value of the swap to the bank.\nFixed leg: \\(100{,}000{,}000\\times0.05\\times(0.9620+0.9230)=\\$9{,}425{,}000\\). Floating leg: \\(100{,}000{,}000\\times(1-0.9230)=\\$7{,}700{,}000\\). Value to the receiver of fixed: \\(9{,}425{,}000-7{,}700{,}000=+\\$1{,}725{,}000\\). The current two-year swap rate is \\(0.0770/1.8850=4.085\\) percent, below the 5.00 percent the bank receives."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 7, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — Exercises Exercises", "body": "7.6 Explain why the swap rate for a given maturity equals the par yield for that maturity on the risk-free curve.\nThe floating leg together with a payment of the principal at maturity is worth the principal, \\(L\\). The fixed leg together with the principal at maturity is a fixed-rate bond with coupon \\(s\\). The swap has zero value when the two are equal, that is, when a bond with coupon \\(s\\) is worth \\(L\\): the definition of the par yield. Algebraically, \\(L=Ls\\tau\\sum d_i+Ld_n\\) gives \\(s=(1-d_n)/(\\tau\\sum d_i)\\).\n7.7 Under a currency swap with three years remaining, a company pays 2.0 percent per year on 1,200 million yen and receives 5.0 percent per year on $10 million, with annual payments and a final exchange of principal. The exchange rate is 125 yen per dollar. Risk-free rates are 4.5 percent in dollars and 1.0 percent in yen, with continuous compounding. Find the value of the swap to the company in dollars."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 7, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 7, Swaps — Exercises Exercises", "body": "Dollar leg: \\(0.5e^{-0.045}+0.5e^{-0.09}+10.5e^{-0.135}=0.4780+0.4570+9.1740=\\$10.1090\\) million. Yen leg: \\(24e^{-0.01}+24e^{-0.02}+1{,}224e^{-0.03}=23.761+23.525+1{,}187.825=1{,}235.111\\) million yen, or \\(1{,}235.111/125=\\$9.8809\\) million. Value: \\(10.1090-9.8809=+\\$0.2281\\) million.\n7.8 A company with a low credit rating borrows for five years at a floating rate, renewed every six months, and enters a five-year swap to pay fixed. State precisely which risks it has removed and which remain.\nIt has removed its exposure to the general level of interest rates: changes in the reference rate on the loan are offset by the swap. It retains (i) the risk that its own credit spread over the reference rate is increased at a renewal, which the swap does not offset; (ii) the risk that the lender declines to renew, leaving it with a swap and no loan; and (iii) counterparty risk on the swap whenever the swap has positive value to it, unless the swap is cleared or collateralized. A five-year fixed-rate loan would have removed (i) and (ii) as well, which is why it costs more."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
